[Applause]  as coronavirus rampaged around the world  britain could see what was coming but  was still unprepared  a year on the failures in hospitals and  care homes are familiar  but even in that deadly spring the uk  was placing its bets  in a great vaccine gamble that today  is paying off the vaccine effort has  galvanized  science business regulators and the nhs  behind the single goal  of saving lives but its success began  with a small group of experts thinking  not like public health officials  but like investors deciding which of  more than 100 candidate vaccines  to back with hundreds of millions of  pounds of public money  they placed their bets on seven  committing to buy hundreds of millions  of doses  before they knew if they worked  remarkably six do  five have completed clinical trials  three are licensed for use and two  have already been administered to 15  million people  steve bates was part of the team are you  surprised at how many of those bets have  come in  i'm amazed i genuinely thought that  there was a decent chance that we'd be  talking at this stage about why we'd  spend  lots and lots of money on things that  didn't stand unearthly of making any  difference whatsoever  we were betting on this before we knew  whether the clinical outcomes were  strong  in some senses that's a nonsensical  approach  [Music]  the team moved fast striking early deals  for the homegrown oxford astrazeneca  vaccine  and with pfizer beyond tech the first  jab to prove its effectiveness  and the uk the first country to  administer it  every step of the way there's been a  collaboration  that has um been defined by  speed agility and rapid decision making  so no lengthy bureaucratic processes  in any organization on our part on the  government  on nhs or the regulator and it's been  unprecedented  what if we now apply that to cancer what  if we apply that to alzheimer's  when small u.s biotech firm novovax  delivered hugely promising results  the uk vaccine team were quick to offer  funding and support  would you consider you know working with  us in uk  that would be manufacturing use our  clinical trial network which  i knew was very good before the call and  maybe we'll fund you know fund this  activity so  so the answer was yes yes yes and that's  been a fantastic collaboration  it's been really enabling to work with  the uk  and uh they they like our technology so  um i think this has been a grand success  distribution has been a triumph of local  nhs  networks gps nurses and volunteers like  here  at cheltenham fire station they're ahead  of national targets and are learning all  the time  they dose patients standing up where  possible to save disinfecting chairs  and the teams compete to be the most  efficient every day  we do 120 patients an hour of six pods  and we're working 12 hours a day so as  long as the vaccine supply is there  we can vaccinate 7000 a week it is the  most humbling experience  any of us have ever had because we're  making such a difference to people's  lives  the vaccine team is already looking  ahead signing a deal with german company  cureback targeting new variants  the uk will long weigh its coveted  failures  what went right with vaccinations may  offer more positive lessons  for the future paul kelso sky news

